      "type": "User",
      "user_view_type": "public",
      "site_admin": false
    },
    "body": "I found that `MiscUtils.sha1` computes the note-modification hash with `SHA-1`, and `NoteViewModel` uses it for both the original hash and later comparison. That violates the `MessageDigestSpec` usage rule: it accepts `SHA-256`, `SHA-384`, `SHA-512`, `SHA256`, `SHA384`, and `SHA512`, not `SHA-1`.\n\nI renamed the helper to `sha256` and updated the note hash caller to use `SHA-256`. The change preserves the existing first-64-bit derivation and exception fallback while keeping the comparison in process. This change removes and renames `MiscUtils.sha1` to `MiscUtils.sha256` and migrates its caller instead of retaining the SHA-1 helper.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime checker for how apps use the Java crypto and TLS APIs.\n\nThe project builds with this change (`:app:assembleFdroidDebug`).\n\nI'm a Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB), working on a research project on Android app analysis. Happy to adjust this to the project's conventions.",
